      "text": "First, we substitute $x$ with $\\frac{1}{x}$ to obtain a second functional equation:\n\\[f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) + 2f(x) = \\frac{3}{x}.\\]Next, we subtract the first functional equation from the second equation to get\n\\[f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) - f(x) = \\frac{3}{x} - 3x.\\]Now, we substitute $x$ with $\\frac{1}{x}$ in the equation $f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) - f(x) = \\frac{3}{x} - 3x$ to get\n\\[f(x) - f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\frac{3}{x} - 3x.\\]Adding the equations $f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) - f(x) = \\frac{3}{x} - 3x$ and $f(x) - f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\frac{3}{x} - 3x,$ we get\n\\[f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) - f \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{3}{x} - 3x + \\frac{3}{x} - 3x,\\]which simplifies to $\\boxed{0}.$ Intuitively, if $k \\neq 0$, $f(k) = f \\left(\\frac 1k\\right)$ and vice versa. Hence, you can plug sexactly any number or $1/s$. Since the identity problem is just solving $\\dots = \\dots$, it's easy to see that there's no\nextra solutions.",
