      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We want to find $f(x)$ for all non-zero $x$ and then set it to $f(-x)$ and see what we get.  We are told $f(x) + 2f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 3x.$  Let's set $x = 1/x.$  This gives $f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + 2f \\left( x \\right) = \\frac{3}{x}.$  We already know what $f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)$ is in terms of $f \\left( x \\right),$ so we get\\begin{align*}\n2f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) & = \\frac{3}{x} - f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\\\\n3f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) & = \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) & = \\frac{1}{x}.\n\\end{align*}Furthermore, we can use our original equation to get \\begin{align*}\nf \\left( x \\right) + 2f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) & = 3x \\\\\nf \\left( x \\right) + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} & = 3x \\\\\nf \\left( x \\right) & = 3x - \\frac{2}{x}.\n\\end{align*}Plugging $\\frac{1}{x}$ into $3x - \\frac{2}{x},$ we get \\begin{align*}\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) & = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\\n& = 3x - 2x \\\\\n& = x.\n\\end{align*}Therefore, $\\boxed{0, \\pm \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}}$ are our real solutions. The answer is 0,\\pm\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}",
